  "content": "To solve the expression $(a \\cdot b + c^3) - (a - b) \\cdot c$ with $a=137$, $b=29$, and $c=11$, we will break down the calculation into intermediate steps using integer arithmetic.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the product $a \\cdot b$\n$$ a \\cdot b = 137 \\cdot 29 $$\n\nWe can compute this as:\n$$ 137 \\cdot 29 = 137 \\cdot (30 - 1) = (137 \\cdot 30) - 137 $$\n$$ 137 \\cdot 30 = 4110 $$\n$$ 4110 - 137 = 3973 $$\n\nSo, **$a \\cdot b = 3973$**.\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the cube $c^3$\n$$ c^3 = 11^3 $$\n$$ 11^2 = 121 $$\n$$ 11^3 = 121 \\cdot 11 = 1331 $$\n\nSo, **$c^3 = 1331$**.\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the first parenthesis $(a \\cdot b + c^3)$\n$$ a \\cdot b + c^3 = 3973 + 1331 $$\n$$ 3973 + 1331 = 5304 $$\n\nSo, **$(a \\cdot b + c^3) = 5304$**.\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the difference $(a - b)$\n$$ a - b = 137 - 29 $$\n$$ 137 - 29 = 108 $$\n\nSo, **$(a - b) = 108$**.\n\n### Step 5: Calculate the product $(a - b) \\cdot c$\n$$ (a - b) \\cdot c = 108 \\cdot 11 $$\n$$ 108 \\cdot 11 = 108 \\cdot (10 + 1) = 1080 + 108 $$\n$$ 1080 + 108 = 1188",
